obi-wan kenobi is one of the most iconic  characters in the star wars universe but  who knew he owes his origins to a  certain late night talk show host keep  watching to discover the untold truth of  obi-wan kenobi  fans are often divided by george lucas's  late career contributions to the star  wars franchise in some ways it can feel  like he's both the best and worst thing  to ever happen to the saga and for a  long time at least whatever lucas said  about his universe would quickly become  law even if he was joking at the star  wars celebration 5 in 2010 lucas was  being interviewed by jon stewart who  observed that while we know about the  homeworlds of most of the saga's main  characters obi-wan kenobi's is a mystery  so he posed the question just where does  obi-wan hail from at this point lucas  paused to think spurring stewart to  insist that he not just make up his  answer lucas sarcastically shot back  that he'd never make something like this  up then proclaimed that obi-wan's  homeworld is the planet of stujon an  obvious amalgamation of jon stewart's  name while this was obviously an offhand  joke for lucas the nature of lucas's  influence on star wars means it's now  enshrined in canon if you visit  obi-wan's page on the official star wars  site you'll even find that stu john is  listed as his homeworld on his databank  profile  in the star wars universe there's  something to be said about the bond  between a padawan and a master as jedi  are instructed to forsake their  emotional ties and relationships a  padawan's closest familial bond becomes  the one who instructs them in the ways  of the force as a result there's a kind  of lineage that can be spoken of in the  ranks of the jedi one that transcends  genetic relationships why do i get the  feeling you're going to be the death of  me  don't say that master  you're the closest thing i have to a  father  obi-wan kenobi is perhaps the most  pivotal and influential jedi master in  star wars  not only did he train the greatest sith  lord in the galaxy his own skills and  training can be traced back to the  ancient era of the high republic during  this time the thriving republic was  expanding to the outer territories  within the galaxy and the jedi were at  their very peak how does this work well  in the empire strikes back it's implied  that yoda trained obi-wan but the  prequel trilogy makes it quite clear  that the bulk of his training came from  qui-gon jinn it's later revealed however  that jin was trained by count dooku who  was in turn trained by yoda so no matter  how you spin it obi-wan's skills are  derived from yodas and yoda learned his  powers as a padawan nearly a millennium  prior during the high republic era it's  one heck of a long line and one that  stretches not just into the past but far  into the future too  obi-wan's journey from the galactic  republic to the galactic empire is  driven by his incessant need to do the  right thing and live by the jedi code in  the prequel films he often appears to  harp on anakin for acting outside the  tenets of the jedi and he rarely wavers  in his loyalties to the jedi council  there is however one notable exception  to this  sadly only one film in the saga portrays  the relationship between obi-wan and his  master qui-gon jinn but it's a  fascinating one nonetheless  throughout the phantom menace obi-wan  objects to his master's decision to  bring anakin skywalker into the jedi  fold but he supports qui-gon regardless  qui-gon meanwhile constantly challenges  the council and is clearly unwilling to  fall in line behind their agenda  especially when they deny his request to  have anakin trained sticking to his  convictions qui-gon commits to teaching  anakin in his dying breath qui-gon asks  obi-wan to train anakin insisting he is  the chosen one risking his standing  among the jedi council obi-wan remains  loyal to his dying master's request and  assumes mentorship over the young  padawan despite his own feelings and the  council's original decision obi-wan's  loyalty to qui-gon jinn clearly runs far  deeper than his own commitment to the  jedi at one point he even tells yoda  that he'll go against the council's  wishes if he doesn't have their support  just like qui-gon it's a stirring  testament to the incredibly close  relationship between obi-wan and his  former master jedi knights are known for  their prowess in the use of the force  and obi-wan kenobi became a legend among  them by defeating darth maul in the  phantom menace of course darth maul  would return to skulk in the shadows for  years to come but as far as the prequels  go their battle is as important as it is  dramatic at the time of their duel a  sith lord had not been seen in nearly a  thousand years defeating and seemingly  killing one solidified obi-wan's  position among the top ranks of the jedi  and he was granted the rank of jedi  knight for accomplishing this feat the  sith are capable and dangerous foes to a  jedi for a reason as they're less  constrained by the moral platitudes of  the jedi order simply put defeating a  sith lord is no mean feat even for a  jedi master obi-wan's victory over darth  maul given his relative youth and  inexperience is one of his greatest and  most under-appreciated accomplishments  while the duel between obi-wan and darth  maul marks the climax of the phantom  menace the two would be destined for  many more rounds via the franchise's  animated shows  on at least three occasions in the clone  wars and rebels obi-wan and darth maul  have been reunited in combat it's soon  revealed that darth maul's connection  with the force as well as his intense  hatred for obi-wan kept him alive after  being severed in two  eventually he obtained robotic legs that  enabled him to once again operate like  normal however he grew resentful towards  the jedi for taking away his future at  palpatine's side maul's brother savage  oppress soon located him and the two set  off on their own path toward building a  criminal empire  ultimately just prior to the events of a  new hope darth maul discovers that  obi-wan is hiding on tatooine and  deduces that he must be serving a  purpose there or protecting somebody he  hunts down his old enemy but after a  brief clash obi-wan delivers him a fatal  wound in maul's dying breath he asks  obi-wan if the one he protects is the  chosen one obi-wan confirms this and  maul dies believing that this chosen one  will avenge them both clearly maul  always knew the sith were his true  enemies having stolen him as a child and  indoctrinated him in the ways of the  dark side and while luke skywalker  didn't avenge maul in quite the way he  might have thought obi-wan's mission to  protect and train the young jedi  nevertheless delivered justice for the  embittered ex-sith lord  the jedi ahsoka is known for many things  one being that she was the only jedi  apprentice of anakin skywalker prior to  his turn to the dark side of the force  her quest to becoming a jedi begins in  the clone wars animated series as anakin  trains the young padawan into becoming a  fully-fledged jedi knight most of the  time obi-wan anakin and ahsoka operate  together on missions and obi-wan is  typically on hand to aid anakin in his  efforts to train his apprentice  likewise ahsoka recognizes obi-wan's  status in the jedi order often going  directly to him to seek guidance and the  two forge a unique bond tragically  following order 66 ahsoka and obi-wan  never made contact again as they're both  forced into hiding from the empire it's  quite likely that he never even knew  that she survived the purging of the  jedi  while obi-wan may be a legendary jedi  knight he isn't infallible most of his  shortcomings are due to the rigid  traditions of the jedi order that  obi-wan adheres to with precision  indeed perhaps his most destructive flaw  is his aversion to matters of the heart  the jedi have always denied their  emotions and romantic attachments to  others but this makes them more  susceptible to volatility and  instability the result  this  [Music]  obi-wan is particularly adherent to this  rule and it even prevents him from  discussing his padawan's obvious  attraction to padme had this been an  open topic for discussion perhaps  obi-wan could have circumvented  palpatine's efforts and reasoned with  anakin before tragedy struck likewise  his fear of impacting the fate of the  galaxy by bringing up family trauma  prevents him from telling luke the  identity of his real father in return of  the jedi obi-wan counsels luke to kill  his father a task luke doesn't think he  can perform obi-wan then laments a  failure that hadn't even occurred  of course luke's love for his father  later brings about the downfall of the  empire proving that the jedi and obi-wan  himself were very much in the wrong  in a 2015 run of star wars comics  published by marvel luke discovers  obi-wan's old journals issue number  seven written by jason aaron details an  adventure in which he rescued luke from  jabba's enforcers as a young boy at the  time luke had become angered by jabba's  henchmen collecting water tax from the  moisture farms  after attempting to steal the water back  luke was caught and came very close to  being sold into slavery  luckily for the intrepid little  do-gooder old ben kenobi had been  watching his back from the shadows  obi-wan leapt into action using the  force to dispatch luke's captors the  next morning luke awakened to aunt peru  asking him what had happened he stated  that he simply could not remember  this would seem to imply that ben kenobi  used the force to wipe luke's memory of  the event true to his word and purpose  this wasn't the only time that obi-wan  has demonstrated his watchful eye and  protective actions over luke  the most famous of these of course  occurs in live action when he scares  away the tuscan raiders in a new hope  and there can be little doubt that he'll  get the chance again when obi-wan kenobi  airs on disney plus in 2022  building an evolving story that grows  both backwards and forward can be  extremely difficult work for george  lucas modernizing old plot points  eventually became a tug of war with  simply telling solid stories leading to  some substantial plot holes in his  franchise one such trouble spot comes  from obi-wan apparently having forgotten  r2d2 and c-3po from his days as a young  jedi knight despite all the adventures  they shared together when alec guinness  sees the droids in a new hope he barely  even acknowledges them well then the joy  does belong to you i don't seem to  remember ever owning a drawing one fan  theory argues that obi-wan may have  voluntarily erased any memory that  connected him with the dissidents of the  empire in order to protect himself and  luke it's a nice idea but nothing exists  in canon to support it and for now fans  will have to assume that in a new hope  old ben kenobi is simply showing his age  at first pass it might seem odd that han  and leia named their son after the name  obi-wan took in exile after all han  barely knew ben kenobi sure the old man  had tagged along with luke aboard the  millennium falcon but he died at the  hands of darth vader mere hours later  after the crew was pulled aboard the  death star obi-wan engages in few  notable interactions with solo and even  leia knew him primarily from his  reputation that said both of them were  aboard the death star and if not for  obi-wan's diversion and sacrifice the  whole group might have been killed  most likely as the years went by luke  shared obi-wan's stories with his sister  and brother-in-law perhaps suggesting  that ben kenobi had helped bring the two  of them together suddenly the name ben  makes a little more sense for the child  of han and leia it's just a shame what  happened next  check out one of our newest videos right  here plus even more looper videos about  your favorite movies are coming soon 